Conclusion: this is a forced session-valid/VBUS bootstrap workaround after
removing the charger/extcon path, not a demonstrated requirement of fixed
peripheral mode. The removal is a plausible explanation for losing the
normal detection path; native BSV, wiring and PMIC detection were not measured,
so the root cause is not closed. A proper connector/VBUS provider or a justified,
device-scoped kernel-glue fix should replace it before claiming general support.
Do not keep usb-role-switch but simply remove the userspace write: that would
leave the new software cable state unasserted. No new kernel/DT/runtime change
or device boot was made during this review.

## MODULES=n and deferred probing

The pinned dd.c:260-264 defaults the timeout to 10 with modules and zero
without. dd.c:291-294 unconditionally returns -ENODEV after initcalls for a
no-modules kernel, even when a positive timeout was explicitly requested.
The RPM SMD channel discovers and populates provider devices asynchronously;
all drivers being built in does not mean all their devices already exist.

The recorded controlled re-probe of unbound 7824900.mmc after RPMPD bound
found HS400 eMMC and all 48 GPT partitions. Patch 0007 permits -EPROBE_DEFER
while a positive requested timeout remains instead of abandoning the
consumer at that point. The actual helper passed a compiled modules on/off,
pre/post-initcalls, timeout -1/0/5 matrix in the original bring-up.

This is narrower than making no-modules behavior equivalent to modules:
dd.c:356-372 still calls fw_devlink_drivers_done and fw_devlink_probing_done
at the late initcall with MODULES=n. Patch 0007 does not change those calls.
core.c:1767-1811 shows the corresponding link relaxation and sync_state
processing; the strict/timeout sync-state policy is separately configurable.

